.TH RIG 1 "2026-10-02" "sc-fleet 0.1.0" "sc-fleet manual"
.SH NAME
rig \- what this box is, and whether it is current
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fBrig\fR
\fBrig status\fR [\fB-a\fR]
\fBrig sync\fR [\fB--dry-run\fR] [\fB--fleet\fR]
\fBrig push\fR [\fB-m\fR \fImsg\fR] [\fB--dry-run\fR]
\fBrig doctor\fR [\fB--disk\fR [\fIpath\fR]]
\fBrig boxes\fR [\fB--lint\fR | \fB--stanza\fR]
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Nothing knew what was true on which box. On one morning a report invented
four dead pipelines out of job logs under a mount that box does not have; a
link checker called nine unmounted citations rotted; a fix that was pushed
read as unpushed because the work was on the other machine; and two capacity
walls were hit in a day. All four are the same missing question.
.PP
\fBrig\fR answers it, and can bring the box you are sitting on back into
line.
.SH THE TWO RULES
.SS Git remotes are the authority
Every box is a replica reconciling to its own upstream. "In sync" means clean
and level. Divergence is surfaced and \fInever\fR merged automatically,
because a machine cannot know which side you meant.
.SS Read all, write local by default
Every box in the roster can be read over ssh. Only the box you are sitting on
is changed, unless you ask for otherwise in as many words with
\fBsync --fleet\fR, which is a flag, never a default, and runs sequentially.
\fBpush\fR is the one verb that writes past this box, and it writes only to
this box's own upstreams, only as a fast-forward.
.PP
The asymmetry between \fBstatus\fR and \fBsync\fR follows from this: reading
with no roster degrades to this box and says so, while writing with no roster
refuses, because a write has to know whose box this is.
.SH WHAT RIG DOES NOT DO
The idempotent installers a site already has are delegated to verbatim, each
named by a config key (see CONFIGURATION): a fast-forward puller for the
config repos (\fBrig.pull\fR, with its own \fBDIVERGED\fR classification), a
journal sync (\fBrig.journal\fR), a link installer with a \fB--check\fR mode
(\fBrig.installer\fR), and a timer installer (\fBrig.timer_installer\fR, or
\fBrig.cron_installer\fR where there is no systemd user manager). \fBrig
sync\fR sequences them. A key that is unset is a skipped stage, never a
finding.
.PP
It adds exactly two things none of them had: noticing that the link manifest
has \fIgained an entry\fR, and reconciling the expected timer set against the
actual one.
.PP
It also does not duplicate a fleet-health monitor. A monitor answers "is the
fleet healthy" across every host; \fBrig\fR answers "is my tooling current on
the boxes I work on", which is a much smaller set.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS rig
This box: per-repo state against its upstream, declared mounts, timers with
their last run, free space and available memory.
.PP
With no roster this still works. Repos are discovered by walking one level
under \fB$HOME\fR and \fB$XDG_CONFIG_HOME\fR for a checkout with a configured
upstream, deduplicated by real path so a repo reached through a symlinked
alias counts once. Absence of data never manufactures a finding: with no
declared timer set, no timer is reported missing.
.SS rig status -a
Every box in the roster, probed read-only in parallel and rendered in roster
order so the output is stable run to run. A box that times out, refuses or
returns a truncated stream is marked transient and does not abort the sweep.
A box marked \fBown=no\fR is skipped unless \fBRIG_INCLUDE_UNOWNED\fR is set;
a box with no \fBssh=\fR alias is listed as registry-only and never contacted.
.SS rig sync [--dry-run]
Bring this box current. \fB--dry-run\fR reports what each stage would do and
changes nothing.
.PP
Order, and why: repos first, because stages 3 to 5 must apply the link list
and unit files that the pull just brought in. A repo that diverged skips
\fIits own\fR dependent stages only, not the whole run, since refusing
everything over one divergence is how a box goes stale for a week.
.PP
.RS
.nf
1  repos       rig.pull
2  journal     rig.journal
3  links       rig.installer --check, then apply
4  dots        the dots installer's dry run, then apply
5  timers      rig.timer_installer, or rig.cron_installer
6  verify      re-run the cheap checks and report on artifacts
.fi
.RE
.PP
Stage 6 reports on \fIartifacts\fR, not on the stages' own exit codes,
because a stage that claims success while the re-check disagrees is precisely
the failure this is meant to catch.
.PP
The dots installer is located by resolving \fI~/.zshrc\fR to its repository
rather than by name, so no directory name is baked into the tool.
.SS rig sync --fleet
Run \fBrig sync\fR on every owned box in the roster, including this one.
.PP
This is the one place \fBrig\fR writes to a box it is not sitting on, so it
is a flag and never a default. It runs \fIsequentially\fR, because these are
writes and a parallel mistake reaches every box at once. A box marked
\fBown=no\fR is refused exactly as it would be locally, a box with no
\fBssh=\fR alias is skipped, and a failure on one box never stops the rest.
.PP
Nothing is reimplemented remotely: each box runs its own local \fBrig
sync\fR, so the stages, refusals and exit contract are identical everywhere.
.PP
A box that does not have \fBrig\fR yet is bootstrapped first: a
fast-forward pull of its own checkout, then that checkout's installer, then
the sync. The pull comes first because a box being bootstrapped is by
definition behind, and its older installer would link only the tools it knew
about.
.PP
\fB--dry-run\fR composes with it and reports what each box would do.
.SS rig push [-m msg] [--dry-run]
The reverse of \fBsync\fR. \fBsync\fR brings this box level with its
origins; \fBpush\fR publishes what this box did. Every repo the roster
declares for this box is visited: a dirty tree is committed, whole
(\fBgit add -A\fR, so untracked files are included and ignored ones are
not), and a repo that is then ahead is pushed. \fB--dry-run\fR fetches, so
the ahead and behind counts are real, and writes nothing.
.PP
The default message is
.RS
.nf
sync: <box> <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM>
.fi
.RE
which is the grammar a journal sync can share, so one
\fBgit log --grep '^sync:'\fR finds every fleet sync commit in every repo.
One message is computed per run and shared by every repo it commits, so the
commits correlate. \fB-m\fR \fImsg\fR replaces it. The body of each sync
commit is the name-status list of what it staged, so it can be read later
without a diff.
.PP
Order per repo, and why: fetch, fast-forward if behind, \fIthen\fR commit,
then push. Committing first would turn a merely stale box into a diverged
one, and stale is what most boxes are most mornings.
.PP
Divergence follows the first rule: surfaced, never merged here. The dirty
tree is still committed, because a local commit is the durable way to hold
work and \fBgit pull --rebase\fR by hand is then the whole fix, but nothing
is pushed from that repo and the run exits 1. The same applies when a
fast-forward is refused because origin's commits touch files this box edited.
.PP
The journal repo (\fBrig.journal_repo\fR) is delegated to the
\fBrig.journal\fR script, which owns whatever merge and dedup logic a journal
needs and nothing here should duplicate. Its message is its own; \fB-m\fR
does not reach it.
.PP
A roster \fBpush=\fR list narrows what \fBpush\fR may commit, while
\fBrepos=\fR is still what \fBsync\fR and \fBstatus\fR read. Leave a public
repo out of it, so an automatic \fBsync: <box>\fR commit can never put a
host name into its history.
.PP
A repo that is absent on this box, detached, or without an upstream is
reported and skipped. An unfinished merge or rebase is never touched. The
push itself gets five times \fBRIG_TIMEOUT\fR.
.SS rig doctor [--disk [path]]
Read-only, and the one door for dotfile drift. Whether the probe, roster and
the configured installers are present, whether this box is registered, whether the
dots repo's own checks pass, whether the link manifest is clean, and whether
the expected timers are enabled. Diagnoses and names the fix; never repairs.
.PP
Four incidents in one summer were all the same class: a rc file that had
become a plain file, a package the dots alias through that was not there, a
secret that had to be hand-built per host, a theme copied across by hand.
Each gained a checker behind a different door. \fBdoctor\fR runs them all
and answers with one exit code.
.PP
The last three rows are integrations with a dotfiles repo, and each is a
skip when its tool or file is absent:
.PP
.RS
.nf
editor   the dots repo's install-editor-stack.sh --check: shell plugins,
         tree-sitter, nvim vintage, and the cli tools the dots assume
         (bat, fzf, rg). Gaps are listed and exit 1 — the script is
         interactive, so nothing unattended can close them, which is why
         sync reports the same result as a note and doctor as a finding.
theme    dye doctor: the theme bundle's own drift check. Its summary
         line is the contract; the FAIL rows are listed. A box without
         dye is a skip, a dye that dies before its summary is a finding.
secrets  the age-sealed ~/.zsh_secrets the dots carry: age present, this
         box's identity present and mode 600, its public key listed in
         secrets/recipients.txt, the blob present, the decrypted copy
         present, mode 600 and byte-identical. The first failing state
         is the row, and it names the one command that fixes it. A dots
         repo with nothing sealed is a skip. The identity is
         ~/.config/age/dotfiles.key, never the vault-backup key.
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fB--disk\fR [\fIpath\fR] adds a disk triage per filesystem: one niced,
ioniced \fBdu\fR under a time budget (\fBRIG_DISK_BUDGET\fR, 300 s per
root), the largest directories at depths one and two, the junk patterns
worth reviewing (caches, __pycache__, node_modules, virtualenvs, old
backups), and a report. Roots are the roster's \fBmounts=\fR plus home and
/tmp, or the one \fIpath\fR you name. The raw traversal and the markdown
report land under \fI~/.local/state/rig/disk/\fR, never over ssh: doctor is
local. A root under 2 GB free is a finding; a traversal cut short by the
budget says PARTIAL and is not passed off as complete. Nothing is deleted.
.PP
Extra timers, enabled here but absent from the roster, are reported and
\fInever\fR disabled. Some are the system's own, and silently disabling
things is the class of surprise this tool exists to remove.
.SS rig boxes [--lint | --stanza]
The roster: one line per box with its role, alias and ownership.
.PP
\fB--lint\fR checks the roster for IP addresses and dotted names outside
\fBmounts=\fR, because the file may sit in a repository that is shared.
.PP
\fB--stanza\fR [\fIname\fR] prints a paste-ready stanza, for this box or a
named one. It is also what a refusal shows you when the box is unregistered.
.PP
\fB--add\fR \fIname\fR appends that stanza to the roster and lints the
result, which is the whole of adding a new server.
.SH IS THE FLEET INSTALLED THERE
The roster declares which boxes should have the tools; \fBown=yes\fR is the
statement of intent. The probe reports what each box actually has, by listing
its \fI~/.local/bin\fR. The expected set is derived from the
\fB# sc-fleet\fR markers here, the same rule the installer uses, so a new
tool becomes expected everywhere the moment it exists.
.PP
\fBrig status -a\fR therefore prints a \fBfleet\fR line per box: either the
count installed, or which tools are missing and that the box has not been set
up. That gap is what \fBsync --fleet\fR closes.
.SH THE ROSTER
Default \fI~/.config/rig/boxes.conf\fR, plus an optional
\fIboxes.local.conf\fR whose values win per box and key, so a machine can
carry an untracked tweak without editing shared data.
.PP
Stanzas opened by a \fBbox=\fR line, \fBkey=value\fR after it, grep-parsed and
never sourced. \fBbox=@defaults\fR supplies every key a box does not set.
.PP
.RS
.nf
box=@defaults
repos=~/config ~/notes ~/dotfiles ~/src/sc-fleet/main
push=~/config ~/notes ~/dotfiles
timers=notes-sync config-pull
sched=auto
own=yes
profile=work
bootstrap_repo=~/src/sc-fleet/main
bootstrap=install.sh

box=atlas
ssh=atlas
role=dev
mounts=/data
note=one line for a human
.fi
.RE
.PP
Keys: \fBssh\fR (absent means registry-only), \fBrole\fR, \fBown\fR,
\fBprofile\fR (the argument the dots installer requires; guessing it wrong
stamps commits with the wrong git identity, so it is data), \fBsched\fR,
\fBtimers\fR, \fBrepos\fR, \fBpush\fR (what \fBrig push\fR may commit;
default \fBrepos\fR), \fBmounts\fR, \fBnote\fR, and for bootstrapping
\fBbootstrap_repo\fR (where that box's checkout lives; defaults to this
checkout's path under \fB$HOME\fR, expanded on that box, not here) and
\fBbootstrap\fR (the installer inside it; default \fIinstall.sh\fR).
.PP
Deliberately absent: distro, editor version, whether a systemd user manager
exists. All are cheaply probed, and a table of them would be a table of lies
within a month.
.PP
\fBThe roster is site data and is never part of the tool.\fR It names hosts,
so it belongs wherever your private configuration lives, symlinked to the
path above by that repository's installer.
.SH THE PROBE
\fIlib/rig-probe.sh\fR is piped to the far side on stdin and run by
\fB/bin/bash -s\fR, so nothing has to be installed there and a box in the
middle of a broken dotfile still answers. It is read-only in three layers,
layered because any one alone is a promise rather than a property: every git
call passes a read-verb whitelist with \fB--no-optional-locks\fR; it never
fetches, because fetching writes refs; and the test suite greps it against a
command whitelist.
.PP
It therefore reports SHAs and nothing derived. All ancestry arithmetic happens
on the caller, which fetches once locally, giving four verdicts: \fBlevel\fR,
\fBahead\fR, \fBbehind\fR, and \fBunpushed\fR when the SHA is unknown here,
meaning that box holds commits no other box has seen. \fBunpushed\fR is
actionable information, not an error.
.PP
The stream ends with \fBend=ok\fR. That sentinel is load-bearing: a stream
truncated because ssh died mid-write is caught by the missing sentinel, not
by an exit code.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The delegated installers are site facts, so they live in the fleet config,
\fI~/.config/sc-fleet/config\fR (a per-machine \fIconfig.local\fR beside it
wins), and never in the tool. \fBsc config\fR shows the effective values.
.TP
.B rig.pull
Fast-forward puller for the config repos. Its output, and the log named by
\fBrig.pull_log\fR, are read for \fBDIVERGED\fR lines.
.TP
.B rig.journal\fR, \fBrig.journal_repo
The journal sync script and the repo it owns; \fBpush\fR hands that repo to
the script instead of committing it.
.TP
.B rig.installer
Link installer. Must accept \fB--check\fR and print
\fBMISSING\fR/\fBWRONG\fR/\fBNOTLINK\fR/\fBORPHAN\fR lines for gaps.
.TP
.B rig.timer_installer\fR, \fBrig.cron_installer
Install the expected timers, under systemd or cron.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B RIG_BOXES
Roster path. Also read by \fBcrew\fR(1), which shares this roster.
.TP
.B RIG_BOX
Override the box identity. Default \fBhostname -s\fR.
.TP
.B RIG_JOURNAL
The journal repo; overrides \fBrig.journal_repo\fR.
.TP
.B RIG_DOTS
The dots repo doctor delegates to. Default: the repository \fI~/.zshrc\fR
resolves into.
.TP
.B RIG_DYE
The \fBdye\fR binary for the theme row. Default: \fBdye\fR on PATH, then
\fI~/.local/bin/dye\fR; absent means the row is skipped.
.TP
.B RIG_AGE
The \fBage\fR binary for the secrets row; \fBage-keygen\fR is looked for
beside it. Default: \fBage\fR on PATH, then \fI~/.local/bin/age\fR.
.TP
.B RIG_SECRETS_ID
This box's identity. Default \fI$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/age/dotfiles.key\fR.
.TP
.B RIG_SECRETS_OUT
The decrypted copy. Default \fI~/.zsh_secrets\fR.
.TP
.B RIG_SECRETS_BLOB\fR, \fBRIG_SECRETS_RECIPIENTS
The sealed blob and the recipients list. Default under
\fI<dots repo>/secrets/\fR.
.TP
.B RIG_DISK_PATHS
Space-separated roots for \fB--disk\fR, replacing the roster set and any
positional path. For tests and one-offs.
.TP
.B RIG_DISK_BUDGET
Seconds \fB--disk\fR gives each root's traversal. Default 300.
.TP
.B RIG_STATE
Default \fI~/.local/state/rig\fR.
.TP
.B RIG_RUN
Scratch for a fan-out. Default \fI$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/rig-$(id -u)\fR, and
deliberately not under \fB$HOME\fR, so two boxes sharing a home cannot
collide.
.TP
.B RIG_PAR
Concurrent probes. Default 6.
.TP
.B RIG_TIMEOUT
Per-box timeout in seconds. Default 12.
.TP
.B RIG_SSH_OPTS
Appended to every ssh invocation, so a jump host can be added without the
tool knowing one exists.
.TP
.B RIG_INCLUDE_UNOWNED
Probe boxes marked \fBown=no\fR. They are still never written to.
.TP
.B RIG_ART
Force art into a pipe.
.TP
.B RIG_NO_ART \fRor\fB NO_COLOR
Suppress art, or colour.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when everything is fine or only transiently wrong: a lock held, a network
failure, an unreachable box, a dirty tree with nothing pending, a gap found
and closed.
.PP
1 when a human must act: an unregistered box, a box that is not yours, a
diverged repo, an unfinished merge or rebase, a link conflict, an orphaned
link, a missing declared mount, a timer that will not enable, an editor-stack
gap, a theme finding from \fBdye\fR, a secrets identity, blob or copy that is
missing, loose or stale, a filesystem under 2 GB free, a commit
a hook refused, a push the remote rejected, or a stage that claimed success
while the re-check disagreed.
.PP
Nothing else, so \fBrig sync\fR can carry an \fBOnFailure\fR hook without
inventing a new contract.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I bin/rig
The tool, in the sc-fleet checkout.
.TP
.I lib/rig-probe.sh
The read-only probe payload.
.TP
.I ~/.config/sc-fleet/config
The fleet config (see CONFIGURATION).
.TP
.I ~/.config/rig/boxes.conf
The roster. Site data.
.TP
.I ~/.config/age/dotfiles.key
This box's identity for the sealed secrets. One per box, never copied
between them; not the vault-backup key that lives beside it under another
name.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/rig/disk/
Where \fB--disk\fR leaves each root's raw traversal (\fI.du.gz\fR) and
report (\fI.md\fR). Latest run overwrites; the date is inside the report.
.TP
.I tests/test_rig.sh
The test suite. Run: \fBbash tests/test_rig.sh\fR
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
.RS
rig                    # is this box current
rig status -a          # is the fleet current
rig sync --dry-run     # what would it change
rig push               # commit what is dirty here, push what is ahead
rig push -m 'wt: fix'  # the same, with a message of your own
rig doctor             # what is misconfigured here, one exit code
rig doctor --disk      # the same, plus a du triage of every mount
rig doctor --disk /tmp # one filesystem only
rig boxes --lint       # is the roster safe to share
.RE
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sc (1),
.BR crew (1),
.BR arm (1),
.BR wt (1)
